WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.250
Film Courage: When you write screenplays with
Jeff [Jeff Haskett-Wood - co-collaborator],

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.639
who usually comes up with the idea?

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:05.440
Ryan Harris, Filmmaker: A lot of times it’s
me.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:12.930
I come to him with a bunch of crap and he
goes…he looks it over and goes Okay…let’s

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:19.350
take this piece and then it becomes more of
a back and forth version with each other.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.070
He does come up with a lot of great ideas.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.940
He has a lot of stories that he wants to tell.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.550
A he’s really good about if he just wants
to write a script, he goes and does it and

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.230
then he’ll pass it to me and we’ll read
it over and I’ll throw in my two cents and

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.230
we’ll start.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:40.340
I feel like we work really well together in
that aspect but it’s definitely I am the

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.480
one who pokes and prods him a lot more to
be like Hey let’s do this or let’s do

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.840
that and he patiently deals with me.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.300
Film Courage: Where do these ideas come from?

00:00:52.300 --> 00:01:01.470
Ryan: A lot of them just stem from stories
that I would love to see as a filmmaker, that

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.190
I would love to go to the movies and watch
and a lot of it comes from movies that I’ve

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:14.490
watched over and over and over again or a
lot of the inspiration comes from movies in

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:15.490
general.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.850
You watch a scene and you think to yourself
that would be really interesting to…even

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.929
a line of dialogue that the character could
say you take that and you go that in and of

00:01:22.929 --> 00:01:24.090
itself could be a story.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.869
That is something that would be interesting
to explore so we just try to find things that

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:41.099
we would love to see as filmmakers and writers
and stories that we would love to 

00:01:41.099 --> 00:02:46.090
be told.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:03:27.239
There 

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.290
is actually a really interesting old Canadian
movie.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:39.389
I say old, the late 90’s at it was called
When Good Ghouls Go Bad and it stars Christopher

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:43.730
Lloyd who is one of the most amazing actors.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:48.560
Everybody knows him as Doc Brown in BACK TO
THE FUTURE but a lot of his stuff is serious

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.780
work and his comedic work is just fantastic.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:58.959
So he has a line where he is speaking to his
son and his son basically says you know you

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:06.529
used to be this go-getter and the town is
dealing with passing of this person who got

00:04:06.529 --> 00:04:13.689
stuck in this kiln overnight and the town
sort of deals with that heavy weight and the

00:04:13.689 --> 00:04:19.120
dad basically says you know you used to be
happy, you used to be a go-getter, you used

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:25.160
to build things and make things and help people
and Christoper Lloyd says yeah and I built

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.660
the kiln that that person died in.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:32.570
It’s one of those moments in that movie
where he didn’t need to say that, that didn’t

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:38.430
need to be a part of the movie, but it was
and that right there you go man that in and

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:45.250
of itself dealing with the trauma of doing
something you love and then that thing you

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.780
love hurts somebody, that in and of itself
could be an incredible story.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:54.360
There are a lot of things in movies and I
try my best to write them down when I feel

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.370
them and see them and I hear them but that
would definitely be an example of something

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:17.120
that 

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.580
has inspired me.

